[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) holds <ProblemCount ticket={4471} /> reports about dates, merged on
subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) and marked known: one issue,
one fix queued. Read by tenant, channel and body, they are three problems, and the queued parser
fix closes only the first. Each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Closing #4471 when the parser fix ships leaves the booking-v3 hint and the one-day moves open,
with nobody assigned to them.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

Counts are built from `reports-4471.json` and `desk-notes.json` at render time, with the desk's own
view: a report the export repeats counts once, under the cause the desk files it under. The one-day
row counts its reports a second time. A tenant who is not on file lands in a "needs triage" row,
not in a problem.

## 1 · The shared parser reads day-first dates month first ([#1](https://github.com/sitek94/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1))

This is the bug #4471 was named for, and it covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" />
reports. Every tenant on file writes the day first, and the parser reads the month first, so
7 April arrives as 4 July. These reports came in through the app, by email, or through the
booking-v2 form. The count includes the one-day moves (problem 3), because the desk files them here.

## 2 · The booking-v3 hint asks for month first ([#2](https://github.com/sitek94/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2))

It covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports, all through the booking form of
tenants that run booking-v3. That form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field; people type what
it asks, and the form reads the value day first. The report reads like a parser report, so the
subject rule merged it. The fix is the hint or the form, not the parser.

## 3 · Dates that move by one day ([#3](https://github.com/sitek94/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3))

It covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day" /> reports: the ones the desk itself listed
as one-day moves in `desk-notes.json`. A Tuesday shows as Monday; one meeting lands on different
days in Lisbon and Toronto. A day/month swap cannot do that. The likely cause is a time zone on the
invite date (not confirmed). The desk files them under `parser` by tenant, so they stay counted
there too; this row is what the queued parser fix will not close.

## What the queue shows

- **The intake tag follows the browser, not the tenant.** Intake tags a report `hint` when the
  reporter's browser is `en-US`. The parser and the hint rows both carry both tags.
- **Same subject, different problem.** Every subject line on the hint and one-day reports also
  appears on parser reports, so the subject rule could not have split them. Read the channel and
  the tenant's form (`causes.ts`) before merging into a big ticket.
- **Keep the one-day list current.** Problem 3 is only as complete as `oneDayMove` in
  `desk-notes.json`. New one-day reports belong there; this page picks them up on the next build.

